+# Team invites — design
+
+Date: 2026-10-01
+Status: approved
+
+## Goal
+
+A team admin can invite someone to the team by email, choosing the role they
+will get. Admins can see the invites that are still waiting and revoke one.
+The invited person receives an email with a link.
+
+Today the only way onto a team is a row inserted by hand, so every new
+teammate is a support request.
+
+## Out of scope
+
+- **Accepting an invite.** The email links to `/invites/accept?token=...`,
+  which does not exist yet. Accepting (sign-up, joining the team, marking the
+  invite `accepted`) is its own spec. The `accepted` status is in the schema
+  now so that spec needs no migration.
+- Resending an invite, and invite history (revoked and expired invites are
+  kept in the table but not shown).
+- Rate limiting and bulk invites.
+- Pointing the mail worker at the new sweep. The worker that already calls
+  `queue.deliver_pending` runs outside this repository; it gains one call,
+  as a deployment follow-up.
+
+## Design
+
+### Data
+
+Migration `005_invites.sql` adds one table:
+
+| Column | Notes |
+|---|---|
+| `id` | primary key |
+| `team_id` | references `teams`, cascade on delete |
+| `email` | stored trimmed and lower-cased |
+| `role` | same three values as `memberships.role` |
+| `token` | unique, `secrets.token_urlsafe(32)`, as password resets do |
+| `status` | `pending`, `accepted`, `revoked` or `expired`; default `pending` |
+| `invited_by` | references `users`, set null on delete |
+| `expires_at` | UTC ISO timestamp, 7 days after creation |
+| `email_queued_at` | nullable; set when the invite email is put on the mail queue |
+| `created_at` | default now |
+
+A partial unique index on `(team_id, email) WHERE status = 'pending'` makes
+"one pending invite per address per team" a database guarantee and not only a
+check in Python.
+
+`app/models/invite.py` follows `app/models/membership.py`: `create`,
+`list_for_team`, `find`, `find_pending_for_email`, `revoke`.
+
+**Expiry.** Status is stored, not computed. `expire_overdue(conn)` flips every
+pending invite whose `expires_at` has passed to `expired`, and each read in
+the model runs it first, so callers never see a stale `pending`. It is one
+indexed `UPDATE`; no scheduled job is needed. Timestamps are written with
+second precision so they compare correctly as text.
+
+### API
+
+`app/handlers/invites.py`, all three endpoints admin only through
+`require_team_role("admin")`:
+
+| Method and path | Result |
+|---|---|
+| `POST /api/teams/<team_id>/invites` with `{"email", "role"}` | `201` and the invite |
+| `GET /api/teams/<team_id>/invites` | `200` and the pending invites, newest first |
+| `DELETE /api/teams/<team_id>/invites/<invite_id>` | `204` |
+
+Validation on create:
+
+- email does not look like an address: `422`
+- role is not one of `membership.ROLES`: `422`
+- the address belongs to someone already on the team: `409`
+- the address already has a pending invite to the team: `409`
+
+Revoke answers `404` for an unknown invite and `409` for one that is no longer
+pending. The token never appears in an API response: it is the invited
+person's credential, not the admin's.
+
+The membership check needs a lookup by email, so `app/models/membership.py`
+gains `find_for_email`.
+
+**The API does not send email.** It only inserts the row. See the next
+section.
+
+### Email
+
+The README rule is that nothing sends mail inside a request, and password
+resets already stretch it by enqueueing in the handler. Invites go one step
+further and keep the handler out of mail entirely:
+
+- `app/mail/invite.py` has `queue_unsent(conn, base_url)`. It finds pending
+  invites whose `email_queued_at` is null, puts the invite email on the
+  existing mail queue for each, and stamps `email_queued_at`.
+- The model gains the two functions the sweep needs: `awaiting_email` and
+  `mark_email_queued`.
+- The template is `app/mail/templates/invite.txt`, in the same format as
+  `password_reset.txt`.
+
+This is what lets the API and the email be built and reviewed separately: the
+only thing they share is the table. An invite created before the email code
+is deployed simply waits with `email_queued_at` null and is picked up by the
+first sweep.
+
+The message is enqueued before the invite is stamped. A crash between the two
+means one duplicate email on the next sweep, which is better than an invite
+that never gets one.
+
+### Screen
+
+Settings > Team gains a "Pending invites" section below the members table,
+for admins only (members and viewers see the page exactly as today):
+
+- a form with an email field, a role select and a "Send invite" button
+- a table of pending invites: email, role, who invited, expiry date, and a
+  "Revoke" button per row
+- an empty state when there are none
+
+The section is its own module, `app/screens/team_invites.py`, rendered by
+`app/screens/team_members.py`, with its behaviour in
+`app/static/team_invites.js`: submit the form to the create endpoint and add
+the row, revoke with a confirmation and remove the row, show API errors in
+the section's alert. `layout.page` currently takes a single script; it
+changes to take several.
+
+## Testing
+
+`unittest`, in the style of the existing suites and using `tests/helpers.py`:
+
+- model: create, duplicate pending, list order and status filter, revoke,
+  expiry (by patching the model's clock)
+- API: each endpoint's happy path, permissions, and every validation rule
+- email: the rendered message, the sweep stamping invites, and a second sweep
+  queueing nothing
+- screen: the rendered HTML for an admin, for a member, and with no invites
+
+The JavaScript has no test harness in this project, as with
+`team_members.js`; it is checked by hand.
+
+## PR Stack
+
+Budget: about 400 review-relevant lines per PR. Tests, lockfiles, generated files and docs are not counted.
+Estimated total: about 550 lines, split into 4 PRs.
+
+| # | PR | Branch | Based on | Est. lines |
+|---|----|--------|----------|-----------|
+| 1 | Add invites table and model | `team-invites/1-schema` | `main` | ~150 |
+| 2 | Invite API endpoints | `team-invites/2-api` | PR 1 | ~100 |
+| 3 | Invite email delivery | `team-invites/3-email` | PR 1 | ~100 |
+| 4 | Invite management screen | `team-invites/4-ui` | PR 2 | ~200 |
+
+Merge order: 1, then 2 and 3 in either order, then 4.
+
+Estimates are anchored on the closest existing code: the memberships model
+and migration (88 lines) for PR 1, with a token, a status and expiry on top;
+the memberships handler (64 lines) for PR 2, with more validation; the
+password reset mail module (15 lines) plus a sweep and two model functions
+for PR 3; the team screen and its script (128 lines) for PR 4, which adds a
+form and builds rows in the browser.
+
+The total would fit in two PRs by size. It is four because the slices are
+read for different things (schema, permissions and validation, delivery
+semantics and wording, markup and browser code), and because PR 2 and PR 3
+do not depend on each other and can be reviewed in parallel.
+
+### PR 1 — Add invites table and model
+- **Delivers:** the `invites` table and `app/models/invite.py` with create, list, find, revoke and expiry. Also carries this spec and the plan.
+- **Touches:** `migrations/005_invites.sql`, `app/models/invite.py`, `tests/test_invite_model.py`, `docs/superpowers/`
+- **Reviewer focus:** the partial unique index for pending invites, and expiring on read instead of with a scheduled job.
+- **Verify:** `python3 -m unittest tests.test_invite_model`, then the full suite.
+- **Safe alone because:** nothing calls the model yet; the migration only adds a table.
+
+### PR 2 — Invite API endpoints
+- **Delivers:** admins can create, list and revoke invites over the JSON API.
+- **Touches:** `app/handlers/invites.py`, `app/server.py`, `app/models/membership.py` (`find_for_email`), `tests/test_invites_api.py`, `tests/test_memberships.py`
+- **Reviewer focus:** the validation order and status codes on create, and that the token is never returned.
+- **Verify:** `python3 -m unittest tests.test_invites_api`, then the full suite.
+- **Safe alone because:** creating an invite only inserts a row. Without PR 3 no email goes out, and without PR 4 nothing in the product calls the endpoints.
+
+### PR 3 — Invite email delivery
+- **Delivers:** `queue_unsent` puts an invite email on the mail queue for every pending invite that has not had one.
+- **Touches:** `app/mail/invite.py`, `app/mail/templates/invite.txt`, `app/models/invite.py` (`awaiting_email`, `mark_email_queued`), `tests/test_invite_mail.py`
+- **Reviewer focus:** enqueue-then-stamp ordering (a crash can duplicate an email, never lose one), and the wording of the email.
+- **Verify:** `python3 -m unittest tests.test_invite_mail`, then the full suite.
+- **Safe alone because:** with no invites in the table the sweep does nothing, and nothing calls it until the worker is pointed at it.
+
+### PR 4 — Invite management screen
+- **Delivers:** the "Pending invites" section on Settings > Team, with the invite form and revoke button, for admins.
+- **Touches:** `app/screens/team_invites.py`, `app/screens/team_members.py`, `app/screens/layout.py`, `app/static/team_invites.js`, `tests/test_team_screen.py`
+- **Reviewer focus:** HTML escaping of invite data on the server, and that the script builds rows with `textContent`, never `innerHTML`.
+- **Verify:** `python3 -m unittest tests.test_team_screen`, then the full suite; by hand, invite and revoke as an admin and load the page as a member.
+- **Safe alone because:** members and viewers see the page exactly as before, and every control in the new section calls an endpoint PR 2 already shipped. It does not need PR 3: invites made from the screen get their email whenever the sweep lands.
